@section [id="abstract"]:
  @heading [level=1]:
    Phase coherence as a marker of dysglycemia in type-2 diabetes
  @paragraph:
    Across [data-type="numeric"]n=412[/data-type] adults
    ([data-type="numeric"]214[/data-type] severe, [data-type="numeric"]198[/data-type] normal),
    cross-frequency phase coherence between continuous-glucose and ECG signals
    distinguished severe dysglycemia from euglycemia
    (p [data-type="pvalue"]= 0.005[/data-type], Mann-Whitney; effect size
    [data-type="numeric"]r = 0.43[/data-type]).

@narrative [role=motivation]:
  Standard glycemic markers (HbA1c, fasting glucose) collapse the dysglycemic
  spectrum to a single number per visit. Phase coherence between glucose and
  cardiac autonomic signals captures the temporal coupling that is lost first
  and recovered last in disease.

@hypothesis [id=H1 status=supported]:
  Subjects with severe dysglycemia exhibit elevated cross-frequency phase
  coherence between glucose and HRV bands compared with euglycemic controls.

@hypothesis [id=H2 status=inconclusive]:
  Coherence rises monotonically with HbA1c within a single subject across
  weeks of CGM follow-up.

@section [id="results"]:
  @heading [level=2]:
    Results

  @result [id=R1 metric=phase_coherence method=mann_whitney
           severe=0.349 normal=0.250 p_value=0.005 effect_size_r=0.43]:
    Cohort-level coherence comparison.

  @result [id=R2 metric=median_lifespan method=log_rank
           treated_days=987 control_days=841 hazard_ratio=0.61 p_value=0.001]:
    Survival analysis on the validation cohort.

  @paragraph:
    Body weight at @metric [name=time value=52 unit=weeks]:
    @metric [name=mass value=28.4 unit=g group=treated]: vs.
    @metric [name=mass value=34.1 unit=g group=control]:
    (p [data-type="pvalue"]< 0.001[/data-type]).

@finding [id=F1 type=primary significance=0.005 validated=permutation]:
  Cross-signal phase coherence separates severe from normal cohorts at a
  population level with a moderate effect size and survives a 10,000-iteration
  permutation null.

@finding [id=F2 type=secondary significance=0.001 validated=bootstrap]:
  The same metric correlates with a 39 % reduction in hazard ratio for
  cardiovascular events at 12-month follow-up.

@link [from=H1 to=R1 type=validated_by]:
@link [from=H1 to=F1 type=supports]:
@link [from=R1 to=F1 type=derived_from]:
@link [from=R2 to=F2 type=derived_from]:

@narrative [role=mechanism_explanation]:
  Sustained autonomic tone is a precondition for tightly-coupled
  glucose-heart phase relationships. Loss of vagal influence under chronic
  hyperglycemia decouples the two oscillators; coherence falls long before
  HbA1c crosses any clinical threshold.

@narrative [role=clinical_implication]:
  A 90-second cohort screen using a wearable ECG patch and a single CGM
  hour could identify pre-diabetic decoupling years earlier than current
  laboratory criteria — without a venous draw.

@narrative [role=limitation]:
  This is observational; coherence is associated with severity but
  causality is not established. The single-cohort validation must be
  replicated on the MIMIC-IV waveform subset.

